\begin{documenttitle}
Applicability of Polyimide Films as Etched-Track Detectors for Ultra-Heavy Cosmic Ray Components
\end{documenttitle}

Tomoya Yamauchi', Kenya Matsukawa', Yutaka Mori', Masato Kanasaki', Atsuto Hattori', Yuri Matai', Tamon Kusumoto', Akira Tao', Keiji Oda', Satoshi Kodaira², Teruaki Konishi², Hisashi Kitamura², Nakahiro Yasuda³, and Rémi Barillon4

1 Graduate School of Maritime Sciences, Kobe University, Kobe 658-0022, Japan

2 National Institute of Radiological Sciences, Chiba 263-8555, Japan

3 Research Institute of Nuclear Engineering, Fukui University, Tsuruga, Fukui 914-0055, Japan

4 Institute Pluridisiplinaire Hubert Curien (UMR7178), Strasbourg Cedex 2, France

Received February 13, 2013; accepted March 15, 2013; published online April 3, 2013

The track registration property in polyimide Kapton has been examined for heavy ions, including 2.3 GeV Fe and 24 GeV Xe ions. Conventional track formation criteria fail to predict the thresholds of etch pit formation, while a chemical criterion stating that etchable tracks are formed when two adjacent diphenyl ethers are broken in the vicinity of the ion's trajectory should be more appropriate. Discriminative detections of ultra-heavy components in cosmic rays, such as Bi, Th, and U ions, are possible by measuring the recorded track length. ©2013 The Japan Society of Applied Physics

S人 ince the polyimide Kapton is known for its ability to maintain excellent physical, electrical, and mechan- ical properties in a wide temperature range from 4 to 673 K, it is an attractive material as a nuclear track mem- brane.1 1-6) 6) Size-controllable nuclear pores have been pro- duced by chemical etching in sodium hypochlorite solution, subsequent to irradiations with heavy ions, including U ions.' 1–3） Such nuclear membranes have been utilized as nanopore membranes, templates for metallic nanowires, aerosol filters, and gas separation films.4–6) Figure 1 shows the chemical structure of Kapton, which is an aromatic polyimide containing a diphenyl ether in each repeat unit. The radiochemical feature of polyimide for ion beams was well summarized by Fink.7)

On the other hand, information of a certain abundance ratio in the ultra-heavy cosmic ray composition spectra, including the ratio of Th to U, can be used to evaluate the validity of modern theories on the origin, acceleration, and propagation of galactic cosmic rays.8–11) In these artificial satellite experiments, etched nuclear track detectors with relatively high detection thresholds have been applied in order to avoid the disturbance by an enormous amount of etch pits by proton and other lighter ions.9,10) In our recent study, we found that the threshold of Kapton was around 25Oo keV/um for Ne and Fe ions at energies less than 6 MeV/n.12) The present study intends to examine the track registration property in Kapton for heavy ions up to Xe ions, as listed in Table I. The validity of the conventional track formation criteria is also checked and it is found to be difficult to predict the track registration behavior of ultra- heavy ions based on them. Studies on modified chemical structure along the latent tracks indicated that the tracks are etchable in Kapton when two adjacent diphenyl ethers are broken in the vicinity of the ion trajectory. This chemical criterion implies that the length of etchable tracks will be significantly different from each other depending on the ion species, such as relativistic Bi, Th, and U ions.

Commercially available Kapton fi lms (from Nilaco) were used throughout the present work. Films of 125 um thickness were used for etching tests in a sodium hypochlorite solution kept at 55 C, which was supplied by Nacalai Tesque, Japan. Its initial content of active chlorine was between 8.5 and 13.5%. The averaged bulk etch rate was 0.71 m/h. The

