ions.' s.17) we could not find, however, any suitable cut-off energies in the case of Kapton. As indicated on the other plot in Fig. 3(b), it seems that Z/β is a better parameter expressing the sensitivity, where Z is the effective charge of the heavy ions and β is the velocity of heavy ions normalized to the light speed in vacuum. 18)

The response of Kapton shown in Fig. 3(b) is close to that of SiO2 glass.19) In the past, the ion explosion spike mechanism was proposed for the track formation in dielec- tric detectors, including polymeric materials, using the value of primary specific ionization (PI), which is considered as the number of ions formed per unit distance along the particle path.20) 0 However, we could not find any possible values of the ionization energy of outer electrons of Kapton, which will work as a single parameter to fit the value of PI on the experimentally obtained thresholds in this model. The conventional track formation models of both REL and PI failed at predicting the track registration behavior in Kapton.

All track registration data are plotted on the graph of the calculated electronic stopping power as a function of β in Fig. 4(a).14) The solid circles indicate the etch pit formation. The open squares signify no etch pit. From the lower energy part with Si ions, the value of 2300 keV /um is derived as the threshold. The Fe ions with 2520 keV/um cannot produce any etchable tracks. These results are concordant with the previously reported one.21) A series of experiments with Xe ions indicates the threshold of 3800 keV/um. The threshold expressed in terms of the stopping power is fairly dependent on the ion species. The identical registration data are plotted on calculated Z/β against β in Fig. 4(b). As for the threshold at lower energies, Z/ β = 165 is suggested, while Z/ β = 150 can be derived from the experiments with Xe ions. The threshold in Z/β is also not an appropriate parameter to extrapolate the discussion simply toward the more heavy ions.

