Figure 5(a) shows the effective track core radius for losses of three different bonds against the stopping power. It was found that the core size for the diphenyl ether bond is generally larger than those for the other two bonds com- posing imide bonds. This means that the diphenyl ether bond is the most radiosensitive part in Kapton. The corresponding track core radius for the threshold of etch pit formation was 1.48 nm, which is compatible with the distance between two adjacent diphenyl ethers in Kapton.12) Two adjacent ) diphenyl ethers could be destroyed in the tracks at the regions of the stopping powers higher than 2300 keV/um. As shown in Fig. 5(b), the G value for loss of carbonyl bond significantly increases at the higher stopping powers. Similar increasing behavior of the G value was found in PET at the relatively lower stopping powers above 300 keV/um, which

