corresponds to the threshold of etchable track formation.22) We found different dependence of the G values on the stopping power in PC and PADC.2.23) The present result is concordant with the previously proposed chemical criterion that the breakings at two adjacent C–O bonds in the radial direction of latent tracks may produce etchable tracks in PET, PC, and PADC.22) A possible damage structure formed at the higher stopping powers in Kapton is more than two breakings at adjacent diphenyl ethers in the vicinity of the ion's trajectory. It is noteworthy that the present results on the track core radius assessed through the FT-IR measure- ments are consistent with the previously reported values as summarized in Fig. 6. The values for C, F, S, and Cu ions were evaluated by electron spin resonance (ESR) spectro- metry. y.24,25) The core size of U ion track was determined ) by scanning electron microscope (SEM) observations after slight etchings.26) We are now working to establish a new experimental system to evaluate the removal cross section for heavy ions with higher energy.

Figure 7 shows the calculated radial dose distribution around ion tracks of 50 MeV Si ion and 1.0 GeV Xe ion, whose stopping parameters are just on each threshold value, and 14.8 GeV Xe ion, which have a stopping power identical to the Si ion.27,28) The longitudinal broken line indicates the effective track core radius of 1.48 nm for the Si ion. The dose of the Si ion at the core radius is significantly higher than that of the 14.8 GeV Xe ion. The dose value at 1.48 nm for the 1.0 GeV Xe ion is close to that of the Si ion. The local dose at the track core radius may be a suitable parameter for expressing the track registration property. The local dose for the U ion at the core radius is lower than that for the 1.0 GeV Xe ion at higher energies less than Z/ β = 150.

The Kapton fi lms should be applied in the form of multi- stacks for the search of cosmic rays. Relativistic heavy ions can be recorded as etchable tracks as far as their stopping parameters were higher than the thresholds for each ion species. As results, the lengths of the etchable tracks depend only on the ion species, in the center region of the stacks. Therefore, it is possible to identify the charge of heavy ions from the total length of recorded tracks and the etch pit size or form on each fi lm.11) Tracks of Fe ions are easily excluded

