Generally, a crystalline material has a higher density than its amorphous counterpart. Therefore, when the crys- talline material is irradiated by ions, its density decreases and its volume increases.20) On the other hand, in the case of an amorphous material, the density of which is originally lower than that of its crystal, the ion irradiation induces densification.13–17) This should also be the case for polymers. In addition, in the case of polymers, chemical bond scission is induced by the ion irradiation, which should enhance densification.21) For these reasons, the dents shown in Fig. 2 were formed as a result of densification at the irradiated fluorinated polyimide surface. In this regard, the increase in refractive index induced by the dent in the ion-irradiated region can be calculated by the following Lorentz–Lorenz equation,22d

\begin{equation}
\Delta n=-\frac{(n^{2}-1)(n^{2}+2)}{6n}\frac{\Delta V}{V},
\end{equation}
\begin{equationid}
(1)
\end{equationid}

where n and ∆n are the refractive index and its increment, and V and ∆ V are the volume associated with the increase in refractive index and its increase, respectively. Here, ∆ V/ V was assumed to be equal to ∆L/L, where L is the projected range of ions and ∆ L is the maximum depth of the dent.

In the case of the proton irradiation at a fluence of 7 × 1016 cm−2, the maximum depth of the dent observed is 320 nm, as shown in Fig. 2(d). On the other hand, when the ion energy is 1.O MeV, the ions penetrate the sample to a depth of 4.0 um, since we used the 16 μm polyimide film to decelerate the ions, as mentioned above. The refractive index of the unirradiated fluorinated polyimide is 1.547, as shown later in Fig. 4. By substituting these values into Eq. (1), ∆n becomes O.053 and the increased ratio ∆n/n becomes 3.4%. The increased ratio of the refractive index was similarly calculated at other fluences, and the results are shown on the right vertical axis in Fig. 3. Figure 4 shows the

