In this study, a polyimide (Pl) thin film is synthesized as a resistive switching layer for resistive random access memory (ReRAM) applications. The experimental results on polyimide thickness show that the Schottky efect between the interface of polyimide and metal thin films is the dominant mechanism in the high-resistance state (HRS). We, therefore, propose a rubbing post-treatment to improve the device performance. Results show that the uniformity and leakage of the memory in the HRS, as well as the power consumption in the low-resistance state (LRS), are improved. The power density of the set process is less than half after the rubbing post-treatment. Moreover, the power density of the reset process can be markedly decreased by about two orders of magnitude. In addition, the rubbed ReRAM exhibits a stable storage capability with seven orders of magnitude lon/lorF current ratio at 85 °C. © 2016 The Japan Society of Applied Physics

\begin{title}
1. Introduction
\end{title}

The physical size of nonvolatile memories (NVMs) has been continually reduced, resulting in an issue of data reliability for the floating-gate structure.1-3) Therefore, many next- generation NVMs are investigated. Among such NVMs, the organic resistive random access memory (ReRAM) has been attracting much attention and is considered as one of the most promising candidates because of its numerous advantages, such as easy process capability, flexibility, low cost, and versatile functionality.4–7) For the ReRAM design, low-power consumption, an appropriate set/reset electric field margin, and high thermal stability are the main considerations in satisfying the practical application requirements. Among numerous polymer types, polyimide (PI) is considered one of the most suitable materials because of its distinct thermal stability, chemical resistance, and mechanical strength.8–10) PI plays a critical role as an alignment layer for production of liquid crystal displays (LCDs). To construct defect-free alignment of LC molecules, rubbing has been widely used in industry. However, because the mechanism of LC orientation on rubbed surfaces is still unclear, there are many studies regarding this matter, taking into consideration various aspects, such as the PI chemical structure and conforma- tion,1 n,11,12) ( rubbing force and density,13,14) ) annealing condi- tion,1 15） 5) combination of rubbing with other techniques,1 16) and the impact of these factors on LCD performance.17–20)

Regarding the resistance-switching layer for ReRAM, PI prepared by a spin-coating method exhibits excellent behav- ior including high Ron/RoFF ratio, superior endurance, low operation voltage, rapid switching, nonrequirement for a forming process, and acceptable retention characteristics.21) However, as device size keeps decreasing, the high power consumption during the set and reset processes and switching energy are the major limitations for ReRAM. Many groups have devoted their studies on power consumption reduction for both inorganic- and organic-based ReRAMs.22-26) More- over, the current International Technology Roadmap for Semiconductors (ITRS) has indicated that the key research challenges for emerging macromolecular memory are the

identification of the switching mechanism and the assessment of key structural elements of macromolecules.27)

To address these defects, first we clarified in this work that the PI-based ReRAM was dominated by the surface effect. A novel PI film, which was synthesized using 4-(4-phenyl- piperazin-1-yl)benzene-1,3-diamine (AP) and 4,4′-oxydiph- thalic anhydride (ODPA), is proposed as a resistance- switching layer for ReRAM applications. Then, a rubbing post-treatment (RP) was applied to PI-based ReRAM to improve the interface effect between the PI thin film and the top electrode. After RP, the set power density was decreased from 9.17 × 102 to 4.34 × 102 w/cm2 and the reset power density was markedly decreased from 5.93 × 104 to 1.13 × 102 w/cm2. To investigate the effect of RP on the device characteristics, samples without RP were fabricated for comparison.

\begin{title}
2. Experimental methods
\end{title}

\begin{title}
2.1 Polyamic acid preparation
\end{title}

Figure 1 shows the process flow for the PI device. A precursor of PI, polyamic acid (PAA), was prepared using AP and ODPA in N2 atmosphere at room temperature. First, ODPA was dissolved in N-methyl-2-pyrrolidone (NMP). Second, AP and NMP were added into the solution after 10 min. Then, the solution was polymerized by stirring for 12 h. The solid content of PAA was 15.24% for both experiments (Exps. I and II), the molecular weight (Mw) was 34374, and the polydispersity index (PDI) was 1.88, which was determined by gel permeation chromatography analysis.

