\begin{title}
2.3 Device fabrication for Exp. Il
\end{title}

In Exp. II, PI thin films with RP were fabricated with interface modification and compared with those samples without RP. As in Exp. I, the samples were prepared on a silicon substrate in an Al/PI/TaN structure. The PI film with a solid content of 15% was spin-coated at speeds from 500 to 250O rpm. After the PAA solution was spin-coated on the TaN electrode, the sample was prebaked at 80 *C for 20 min in N2 atmosphere. Then, the sample was imidized at 260 °C in N2 atmosphere for 30 min. Subsequently, RP was implemented to modify the arrangement of surface molecular chains. Unidirectional rubbing was performed with a rubbing machine, similar to that described by Stoica et al.14) The

PI-coated substrate was put on the platform and moved at constant transport velocity and rubbing depth underneath the rotating cylinder. During the rubbing process, the cylinder was rotated at a constant rotation speed (@) of 400 rpm, whereas the polymer-coated substrate was moved underneath the rotating cylinder at a constant transport velocity (v) of 500 rpm. Samples S1_RP, S2_RP, and S3_RP were stressed using the same force as that during the rubbing process. After one rubbing cycle, the thicknesses of the samples were measured. The measured results of the samples with and without RP are listed in Table I. Finally, a 20O-nm-thick A1 top electrode layer was deposited using metal masks with an evaporator. The diameter of each sample was 100 μm.

