\begin{title}
3. Results and discussion
\end{title}

\begin{title}
3.1 Pl-based ReRAM under surface domination
\end{title}

In this study, typical current-voltage (I–V) curves with both unipolar and bipolar switching phenomena for the PI-based ReRAM were obtained, similar to those for other metal/ polymer/metal devices.28) At the initial stage, the PI-based ReRAM was in the high-resistance state (HRS) and exhibited a forming-free property. That is, the set voltage was initially in the range of those in the later stages. Through the "set process", the device can be switched from HRS to the low- resistance state (LRS) with a current compliance of 5 uA, and the reverse process without current compliance is referred to in this state as the "reset process'". Once the HRS or LRS is achieved, the device remains in HRS or LRS at a read voltage

