To confirm whether the PI-based ReRAM was dominated by the bulk or surface, in Exp. I, the solid content of PAA was fixed at 15% and the PI thin film thicknesses were adjusted by controlling the spin-coating speed. Figure 2 shows relative current versus PI thin film thickness. Regarding the LRS at VREAD of O.1 V, the current increased with decreasing PI thickness because the electron transport mechanism in the LRS operates according to ohmic con- duction, which follows the equation J = nqE, where J is the current density, n is the carrier concentration, q is the electric

charge,  is the mobility, and E is the electric field. When the device dimensions and solid content of PAA are fixed, IoN depends on PI thickness and the number of conduction paths. That is, the LRS property is dominated by the bulk effect. Conversely, the current in the HRS was kept approximately 1 × 10-11 A at VREAD of 0.1 V regardless of whether the PI thickness was 862.44 or 544.99 nm. This phenomenon indicated that the HRS of the PI-based ReRAM was dominated by the surface effect. However, to modify the HRS property by an additional fabrication method is more important than to modify the LRS property, because the LRS property can be controlled by applying various current compliances in the set operation process. Therefore, a surface treatment, RP, was applied in this study for the PI-based ReRAM.

