Received July 18, 2017; accepted September 26, 2017; published online December 13, 2017

Fluorinated polyimide films containing cobalt chloride based on hexafluoroisopropylidenediphthalic dianhydride and 4,4'-diamino-3,3'-dimethyl diphenylmethane were treated by nanosecond pulsed electrical discharges generated in distiled water. The polyimide films have been char- acterized by Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectra and contact angle measurements, optical transmission spectroscopy, and fluorescence spectroscopy. Significant changes in some intrinsic fluorescence features, such as the intensity and position of the emission peak, have been observed during exposure to water plasma. These effects have been considered to correlate with the development of specific chemical interactions between the liquid and the macromolecules, including the formation of hydrogen bridges. A slight increase in surface hydrophobicity was observed after plasma treatment. FTIR spectra showed a decrease in the intensity of the absorption band and an opening of the imide ring, depending on the treatment time. © 2018 The Japan Society of Applied Physics

\begin{title}
1. Introduction
\end{title}

Polyimides (PIs) are high-performance materials widely used as electrical insulators, coatings, and substrates for flexible printed circuits, owing to their low dielectric constant and superior thermal and mechanical properties.1-5) The ability of the polymer to maintain its integrity yet incorporate new and interesting chemical and physical properties when blended with other materials makes it also suitable for a range of other applications. Although PIs have been widely used in micro- electronics and aerospace industries, their apparent color change from yellow to brown has limited their application in optical communication and photonic devices.6–12) The colora- tion of polyimide was mainly attributed to intermolecular charge transfer (CT) complexes, owing to the molecular aggregation between PI chains in solid state. The fluorescence of PIs is also related to the intermolecular packing and CT nature, where aromatic moieties from diamine act as electron donors and imide moieties act as electron acceptors.6) Some authors have reported that the CT fluorescence observed for the models mainly originates from the excited intramolecular CT state.6) However, the insolubility of conventional PIs in organic solvents makes it difficult to distinguish the effects of intra- and intermolecular interactions.5.8) Fully aromatic polyimides are known to exhibit fluorescence within the visible region, but the quantum yield of the fluorescence observed is relatively low owing to the strong CT nature of the polyimides and few studies were dedicated to applications of aromatic polyimides for electroluminescent devices.10-12) On ) the other hand, fluorinated PIs have attracted much attention owing to the decrease in the degree of CT interaction caused by CF3-substituted diamines.12–14) The introduction of hexa- fluoropropanedianhydride (6FDA) to the polyimide main chain derived from dianhydrides weakens the intermolecular CT owing to the bulky substituent group —C(CF3)2—, which prevents the molecular packing of polyimide films.14) In the case of cobalt-modified PIs, note that cobalt(Il) chloride groups may also diffuse preferentially through amorphous regions, forming charge transfer complexes.6)

Recently, it has been found that the surface segregation of CF3 occurs and/or the concentration of unsaturated bonds at

the surface increases when fluorinated PI fi lms are treated by plasmas that are generated in water.14,15) A topographical rearrangement of polymeric chains was also observed after exposure to water plasma. When water is subjected to a high electric field, streamers and shock waves are formed in the interelectrode gap.16–18) Excited species, such as hydrogen, oxygen, and hydroxyl radicals and UV radiation are pro- duced.17,18) Streamer propagation may further lead to more efficient chemically active species propagation and interac- tion with molecules and bulk materials, inducing surface modification and functionalization.

The purpose of this study is to investigate the potential of discharges that are generated with nanosecond high-voltage pulses in distilled water to modify the optical properties of a cobalt-containing aromatic polyimides synthesized by the solution polycondensation of aromatic dianhydride (hexa- fluoroisopropylidenediphthalic dianhydride-6FDA) with aro- matic diamine (4,4′-diamino-3,3′-dimethyldiphenylmethane- MMDA). Contact angle measurements were performed to investigate the surface hydrophobicity of polymer films that were treated with consecutive pulses for 10, 15, and 20 min. The intrinsic optical features of plasma-treated PI fi lms were investigated by optical transmission spectroscopy and fluo- rescence spectroscopy.

\begin{title}
2. Experimental procedure
\end{title}

Pulsed electrical discharges were generated in a cubic quartz vessel between electrodes immersed in 10 ml of distilled water (5 uS cm–1). A tungsten rod electrode of 1 mm diameter and a stainless steel plate of 15 × 15 mm2 size were arranged in a pin-to-plate configuration (Fig. 1). The interelectrode gap was set to 4 mm. The details are presented in our previous works.15,19) High-voltage pulses were applied with a com- mercial nanosecond generator (FID Technology FPG 150- 1NK). The pulse generator can deliver positive high-voltage pulses of rectangular shape with a duration of 10 ns (FWHM) and a rise time of 3 ns to a load of 50 Ω. For polymer film treatments, the output voltage was set to 50 k V and the repeti- tion rate to 10 Hz. For voltage and current measurements, an in-house capacitive voltage divider (CVD) 1:190 and a Pearson current monitor, model 6585 (500 Apik, 250 MHz),

