\begin{title}
3.1  FTIR
\end{title}

The FTIR spectra of polyimide films containing cobalt chloride without and with plasma treatment proved that the characteristic absorption bands of the imide cycle are present in all samples. Figure 2 displays representative FTIR spectra of PI/CoCl2 hybrid films (10% CoCl2) in comparison with those of untreated and plasma-treated films for different periods of time (10, 15, and 20 min). Thus, the characteristic imide ring absorptions appeared in the FTIR spectra at 1720– 1725 cm−1 (symmetrical C=O imide stretching) and around 1375 cm−1 (C–N–C axial stretching), demonstrating the

<chart>
\begin{imagecaption}
Fig. 2. FTIR spectra of untreated and plasma-treated cobalt chloride- containing polyimide films.
\end{imagecaption}

preservation of the polyimide cycle.20-22) The characteristic absorption bands of hexafluoroisopropylidene are present at 1260 and 1210 cm−1. By increasing the plasma treatment time, the intensity of the band characteristic for the imide cycle remained unmodified, except for the 15-min plasma- treated sample. The intensities of the absorption bands at around 1723 (C=O) and 1375 (C–N) for the 15-min plasma- treated sample are lower than those obtained for the untreated and plasma treated films for different discharge times. This might be caused by an opening of the imide ring in the 15 min plasma exposure case and a reconstruction when the discharge time is prolonged to 20 min. Intermolecular bonding process- es, such as hydrogen bonding, may be renewed, together with some other units of the imide cycle (C=O, N–). It may be assumed that highly energetic excited species may reach the polymer surface by means of bubbles and streamers, producing the above-mentioned structural modifications.

A reduction in intensity and the broadening of the absorp- tion bands were observed in the range of 1100–1400 cm–1. The broadening may be caused by an increase in the strength of an intermolecular interaction, such as C–F or hydrogen bonding belonging to benzene rings. The increase in tem- perature may also determine the broadening of the spectral line and the decrease in relative intensity. The added thermal energy during plasma treatment may gradually overcome the intermolecular and intramolecular forces, and less energy is required to excite the vibrational dipoles.

However, based on our FTIR and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPs) 19) results, we may assume that the active species that are formed under the action of ns-plasma treat- ment can initiate the random hemolytic cleavages of carbon— nitrogen and carbon–carbon bonds in imide rings, and carbon—nitrogen bonds in the main chains. These cleavages give rise to chain-free radicals (2,2-hexafluoroisopropylidene diphthalimidy1, N-phthalimidil, 3-methyl-4-amino phenylene, 4-methylen-2-methylaniline), which are recombined and can lead to some characteristic structures (Fig. 3), such as phenyl isocyanate, 2-phenyl-2-phthalimide-hexafluropropane, 2,2- diphenylhexafluropropane, 2,3-dihydro-1,4-phthalazinedione, benzene, and toluene.23)

