\begin{title}
3.3 Optical properties of polyimide
\end{title}

3.3.1 UV–vis absorption spectra. Figure 5 shows the transmittance spectra of the PI fi lms plasma treated for 10, 15, and 20 min. The absorption peaks observed at 538 nm can be assigned to π—π* transitions excited from the imide carbonyl (C=O) to the phenyl ring of 6FDA.24) Cobalt(II) chloride produced the CT band observed at 550–700 nm. The CT band for crystalline cobalt(II) chloride is 670 nm.6) As the humidity increased, the peak intensity decreased. This corre- lates with the transition change of the weak coordination of water molecules to the cobalt ion. A decrease in absorbance was observed upon exposure to plasma for all PI samples, indicating an increase in the number of fluorophore species with the discharge time (Fig. 5). The spectrum obtained was 2-nm-blue-shifted for all the plasma-treated PI films compared with the untreated PI film. This may suggest an ablative process occurring under UV radiation in water plasma. The same effect was observed under photolysis conditions owing to polyimide film exposure to a medium- pressure mercury lamp in air.25) A possible explanation for this behavior is the degree of interchain interaction in the form of CT complexes present in the respective polymers. The strength of these CT complexes depends on the electron affinity of the anhydride moiety and the ionization potential of the diamine moiety.10.25) The electron-withdrawing ability of 6F groups serves to increase the electron affinity of the dianhydride moiety and leads to stronger CT complexes. 6FDA contains a bulky substituent group —C(CF3)2 between two phenylene groups, reducing the electron density of phenylene groups owing to the strong negativity of fluorine atoms. The steric bulk of these groups may prevent an

