3.3.2 Fluorescence spectroscopy. Figure 6 shows the fluorescence spectra of the untreated PI films and water- plasma-treated PI fi lms for 10, 15, and 20 min. When the fi lm was excited at 450 nm, a redshift of the emission peak at 558 nm was observed for the untreated sample, showing a marked excitation wavelength dependence of the emission peak. This bathochromic shift is a special phenomenon that occurs in transparent PI films, compared with the case of con- ventional aromatic PIs.7.26) The emission peak was assigned by Hasegawa and coworkers to the emission of a ground- state intermolecular CT complex, and was considered as due to an increased degree of molecular packing resulting from the annealing process.27,28) They have also stated that the emission from excited ground state complexes is likely to occur since the energy difference between the ground and excited states of each complex is not monodisperse in amorphous solids, and that the redshift of the emission peak occurs according to the shift in excitation wavelength. This bathochromic shift may also be due to the intramolecular hydrogen bonding that changes the electronic state of the main chain π-conjugation.29) The enhancement of intra- molecular CT interaction does not significantly occur, and the spectral change mainly originates from the enhancement of van der Waals interaction.29) The bathochromic shift can be due to the presence of cobalt chloride as was mentioned for other polyimide hybrids that incorporated titania and silica in the macromolecular chain.30)

The fluorescence peak of the plasma-treated PI fi lms shifted to shorter wavelengths than did the peak of the untreated PI film. The emission peak was observed at 557 nm for the 10-min plasma-treated PI film and at 555 nm for the 15- and 20-min plasma-treated PI films (Fig. 6). This may be induced by a hypsochromic shift by 1 to 3 nm and a gradual increase in emission intensity with the discharge time (Fig. 7). 6FDA-based PIs exposed to pure nitrogen showed a marked intensity loss of the florescence peaks when nitrogen loaded with ethanol, isopropanol, and water vapor mixtures were used in the experiment.31) These effects were attributed to the formation of hydrogen bonds between the

