fluorophore and the alcoholic solvent, and between the carbonyl oxygen (CT complex electron acceptor) of the dianhydride moiety of the PI chain and the analyte molecule. The loosened chain packing of macromolecules caused by the permeation of analyte molecules through the polymeric network was presented by the authors as a possible mechan- ism responsible for the emission yield decrease. In our case, water might also be readily adsorbed into the spaces between the chains of the polymer structure, but this cannot explain the increase in the intensity of the fluorescent peak with the discharge time observed after water plasma treatment. XRD measurements for this PI shown previously in other works have also revealed a decrease in intermolecular distance and the strengthening of the π—π interactions.19) Shock waves are generated in plasma.1 a.15.16) It is well known that the variation in pressurization affects the intermolecular and intramolecu- lar CT transitions.29) It may be assumed that intramolecular rather than intermolecular CT complexes are responsible for the increase in the fluorescence response of the water-plasma- treated PI films. The charge transfer in the lowest excited state may also rationalize this blue shift. Recent studies of polymer blends have shown that the Coulomb interaction between the charge distribution of an excited state chain and that of an adjacent neutral chain can lead to a blue shift of the emission in the repulsive regime.32.33) The collapse of intramolecular hydrogen bonding in blended polymers was found to be responsible for the blue shifts of the fluorescence peak.33) It may be assumed that the intramolecular bonding of the PI films has been destabilized and destroyed under water plasma treatment, inducing changes in polymer conforma- tional freedom. The slight blue shift of the emission spectra may be the consequence of the UV radiation formed in plasma,15) which effectively induced the modification at the intramolecular level. The excited species may induce a random hemolytic cleavage of bonds in the main chain, resulting in new structures, such as 2,3-dihydro-1,4-phthala- zinedione. This compound and the new hydrogen bonds created may also explain the blue fuorescence of polyimide films after nanosecond plasma treatment.

\begin{title}
4.  Conclusions
\end{title}

The PI fi lm containing 15% cobalt chloride has been treated by nanosecond pulsed electrical discharges generated in

<pageheader>

<hline>

<pageheader>

\begin{pagenumber}
0102BF-4
\end{pagenumber}

<pagefooter>

distilled water for 10, 15, and 20 min. IR spectra showed no eff ect of the characteristic absorption band of the imide cycle after treatment in plasma generated in water, except for the 15-min plasma-treated sample, in which an opening of the imide ring was observed, followed by a reconstruction when the discharge time was prolonged to 20 min. The plasma treatment has a small effect on the water contact angle of polyimide samples regardless of time. After 10 min of nanosecond treatment, the contact angle increased. The increase in the fluorescence response of the water-plasma- treated PI samples was observed with the increase in discharge time, as a consequence of chain free radicals formed during the plasma treatment. The fluorescence peak of the plasma-treated films shifted to shorter wavelengths than did the peak of the untreated PI film. This may open up the possibility of using such a technique for the generation of PIs with enhanced optical properties.

